ARBOR: A Formally Verified, Staged, Falsifiable Architecture for Distributed Tree-Associated Environmental Sensing, with Convergence Guarantees, Interval-Robust Safety Certification, and Gated Biohybrid Extensions

Author: Ouadi Maakoul · Research collaboration: SI TEAM · Date: 17 November 2026

Status: Theoretical doctoral research proposal. Formal mathematical foundations, quantitative experimental protocols, and mechanised conditional safety proofs. Not experimentally validated. All numerical thresholds are placeholders to be fixed by power analysis and pre-registration before data collection. No threshold is derived from ARBOR data. The system is named ARBOR rather than ARBOR-AI because the safety-critical path is model-based; learned components are tested as augmentations but are not in the certified path.

---

Abstract

This proposal investigates whether low-power electronic agents associated with urban trees can improve ecological monitoring and irrigation decisions, and whether two speculative biohybrid additions—plant-derived bioelectrical energy and plant physiological dynamics used as a physical reservoir—yield measurable benefit over a conventional solar-powered design. The architecture is staged into three tracks: Track A, a solar-powered distributed sensing and irrigation-decision network, which is the deployable baseline; Track B, bioenergy characterisation with a plant-free soil microbial fuel cell control; and Track C, plant-reservoir estimation tested as a strict ablation. An integrated biohybrid node is constructed only if Track B or Track C passes a pre-registered gate and then improves at least one outcome over Track A at comparable cost and maintenance burden.

The formal contribution is a conditional safety architecture. A trust-weighted spatial consensus protocol is analysed as a product of time-varying stochastic matrices; exponential convergence and an input-to-state-stability disagreement bound are established under joint strong connectivity and bounded observation noise. A centralised weighted least-squares comparator operationalises the decentralisation kill criterion. A coupled electro-hydraulic state-space model defines the plant-reservoir ablation. A discrete-time Control Barrier Function, extended by verified interval arithmetic, guarantees forward invariance of the hydrological safe set under bounded sensor uncertainty, reducing worst-case safety checking to two scalar endpoint evaluations via concavity. A type-theoretic governance kernel, mechanised in Lean 4 for the point-valued case and specified with remaining proof obligations for the interval-robust case, makes an actuation command unconstructible unless the policy supplies proof terms for sensor validity, CBF invariance, ecological non-inferiority, and municipal authorisation. The formal results are explicitly conditional on their water-balance and sensor-error models; the field experiment tests the antecedent of those conditional statements, including a pre-registered coupling validation that the CBF safe set is sufficient for ecological safety.

The proposal does not assume that trees compute, that mycorrhizal networks form a communication channel, or that biological energy can power a general-purpose AI system. A negative result for any hypothesis demotes the corresponding component and is reported as such. The communication graph is a radio network among electronic nodes; soil, roots, and fungal networks are not communication edges and are not modelled as such.

