1. Problem Statement and Scope

Let \mathcal{C} denote the set of candidate system configurations, each a triple

c=(e,s,a)

of energy source e, sensing set s, and intelligence architecture a. Let

J(c)=(J_E,J_W,J_M,J_C,J_H,J_K)

be a vector of outcomes: net energy, water applied, estimation error, communication cost, tree-health indicators, and cost/maintenance burden. The principal question is:

Q: Does there exist c\in\mathcal{C} with a biohybrid element (e=\mathrm{bio} or s=\mathrm{plant\text{-}reservoir}) such that, for at least one pre-specified outcome J_k, J_k(c) is better than J_k(c_{\mathrm{conv}}) by a margin \delta_k, where c_{\mathrm{conv}} is the best conventional solar, non-plant-reservoir configuration, at cost and maintenance not exceeding (1+\kappa) times those of c_{\mathrm{conv}}?

The answer may be negative. A negative answer still validates c_{\mathrm{conv}}, which is itself a useful outcome.

Non-claims. The proposal does not claim that (i) trees possess digital intelligence; (ii) mycorrhizal networks are a reliable communication channel; (iii) biological energy can power a general-purpose AI server; or (iv) novelty, which requires a systematic review.

Terminology note. The system is named ARBOR, not ARBOR-AI, because the safety-critical path—sensing, water balance, consensus, CBF, governance kernel—is model-based and contains no learned component. Learned components (the Gaussian-process or weighted least-squares spatial comparator in Track A, the reservoir readout in Track C) are tested as augmentations and are not in the certified path. If a learned component were to enter the certified path, it would require its own safety case, which this proposal does not supply.

