2. Architecture

2.1 Three tracks and an integration gate

Track A (baseline, built first). Solar-powered nodes with capacitive soil moisture, soil and air temperature, humidity, and irradiance sensing, a microcontroller, and LoRa. Decision logic is a calibrated soil-water balance with sensor feedback, rain skip, and a safety envelope enforced by the governance kernel. Coordination is trust-weighted event-triggered consensus over a cluster-head topology.

Track B (sidecar science). Plant microbial fuel cells and, separately, plant-free soil-MFC test cells with identical electrodes and substrate, instrumented for voltage, current, temperature, moisture, and light. Bioenergy does not power the Track A node until it passes the energy gate.

Track C (sidecar science). A small number of sentinel plants carrying leaf-thickness, dendrometer, or sap-flow sensors, plus Track A environmental sensors, used only for the electro-hydraulic reservoir ablation.

Integration gate G. An integrated node is built only if Track B or Track C passes a pre-registered gate and then improves at least one outcome over Track A at comparable cost and maintenance.

2.2 Tree-associated agent

Each node is the tuple

A_i(t)=(E_i,M_i,S_i,C_i,N_i,Q_i),

with E_i stored energy, M_i memory and history, S_i state estimates with uncertainty, C_i computational budget, N_i reachable neighbours, and Q_i\in\{0,1\} a calibration and health flag. Measurements are labelled as direct, proxy, or model-derived. The communication graph is \mathcal{G}(t)=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E}(t)). No permanent central coordinator is required for local monitoring, but valve actuation and city-level policy require authorisation through the governance kernel.

