3. Sensing, Energy, and Calibration

3.1 Energy demand and balance

Per-cycle demand and average demand of node i are

E_{\mathrm{cyc}}
=
E_{\mathrm{wake}}
+\sum_s E_{\mathrm{sense},s}
+E_{\mathrm{cpu}}
+\sum_k\left[E_{\mathrm{tx}}(\mathrm{SF}_k,\mathrm{PL}_k)+E_{\mathrm{rx},k}\right],

P_D=P_{\mathrm{sleep}}+\frac{E_{\mathrm{cyc}}}{T_{\mathrm{cyc}}}.

Receive windows, clock calibration, retransmissions, and regulator leakage can dominate over transmission, so E_{\mathrm{rx}} and sleep current must be measured rather than taken from datasheets. Stored energy evolves as

E_i(t+1)
=
\min\left\{
E_{\max,i},
E_i(t)+\eta_c H_i(t)\Delta t-\frac{D_i(t)\Delta t}{\eta_d}-L_i(t)\Delta t
\right\},

with

H_i(t)=H_{\mathrm{PV},i}(t)+H_{B,i}(t).

The energy-reliability constraint is

\Pr\left[E_i(t)<E_{\min,i}\right]\leq \varepsilon_E
\quad \forall t\in\Omega,

where \Omega covers temperature, irradiance, moisture, and season. The bioenergy share over window W is

\phi_B(W)=\frac{\int_W H_B\,dt}{\int_W D\,dt},

reported as the 30-day mean, the worst 7-day window, and night-time and winter values separately.

3.2 Operational workload and energy gate

The reference workload is defined explicitly. A representative daily active energy budget is:

Operation Frequency Energy per operation (\muJ) Daily energy (\muJ)
Soil moisture measurement 4/day 500 2,000
Soil temperature 4/day 100 400
Air temperature and humidity 4/day 150 600
Irradiance 4/day 100 400
Microcontroller wake and process 4/day 200 800
LoRa transmission 4/day 20,592 82,368
Receive window 2/day 5,000 10,000
Storage write 4/day 50 200
Active daily total   96,768

Thus

E_{\mathrm{cyc,daily}}\approx 96{,}768\ \mu\mathrm{J}=96.768\ \mathrm{mJ}.

The active average power is

P_{\mathrm{active}}=\frac{96{,}768\ \mu\mathrm{J}}{86{,}400\ \mathrm{s}}\approx 1.12\ \mu\mathrm{W}.

The true average power is

P_{\mathrm{avg,node}}=P_{\mathrm{sleep}}+P_{\mathrm{active}}.

The quiescent sleep power P_{\mathrm{sleep}} is not negligible and must be measured. If the measured sleep current is 20\ \mu\mathrm{A} at 3.3\ \mathrm{V}, then

P_{\mathrm{sleep}}\approx 66\ \mu\mathrm{W},

which dominates the active budget. In that case the required source power with a safety factor of 3 is approximately

P_{\mathrm{required}}\approx 3(66+1.12)\approx 201\ \mu\mathrm{W}.

The primary design target is the ultra-low-quiescent path: P_{\mathrm{sleep}}\leq 1.5\ \mu\mathrm{W}, giving

P_{\mathrm{avg,node}}\leq 2.62\ \mu\mathrm{W},
\qquad
P_{\mathrm{required}}\leq 7.9\ \mu\mathrm{W}.

The energy gate is therefore stated in two forms, both fixed by the measured sleep current and the pre-registered safety factor:

· Relative gate: \Delta P_{\mathrm{plant}}=P_{\mathrm{PMFC}}-P_{\mathrm{soil\text{-}MFC}}\geq 0.10\,P_{\mathrm{PMFC}}.
· Absolute gate: \Delta P_{\mathrm{plant}}\geq 20\ \mu\mathrm{W} as a 30-day mean during the growing season, and \Delta P_{\mathrm{plant}}\geq 10\ \mu\mathrm{W} above the soil-MFC control.

The relative gate alone can pass on a very small base; the absolute gate prevents this. Electrode area is fixed at 100\ \mathrm{cm^2} per electrode, giving 0.01\ \mathrm{m^2} of projected area. At the literature-reported power densities of 10\text{--}26\ \mathrm{mW/m^2} for planted PMFCs and 3.7\ \mathrm{mW/m^2} for unplanted controls, this area yields 100\text{--}260\ \mu\mathrm{W} gross and a plant-specific contribution of order 60\text{--}220\ \mu\mathrm{W} at peak—above the absolute gate if sustained. If the measured plant-specific contribution falls below the absolute gate, the claimed plant-specific energy advantage is rejected regardless of the relative figure.

The energy gate therefore uses the measured value:

\bar P_{\mathrm{PMFC}}<P_{\mathrm{required}}
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\text{PMFC is rejected as sole power source}.

3.3 Measurement model and variance propagation

For raw sensor output y_{ij} of sensor type j at node i,

y_{ij}(t)
=
h_j\bigl(\theta_i(t),T_i(t),\mathrm{EC}_i(t);\psi_j\bigr)+e_{ij}(t),

\hat\theta_i(t)\pm\sigma_{\theta,i}(t),
\qquad
\sigma_{\theta,i}^2=s_{\mathrm{cal}}^2+s_T^2+s_{\mathrm{noise}}^2.

Capacitance probes are sensitive to temperature, salinity, and soil type. Raw values, co-located temperature, calibration curve, and calibration date are logged. The degradation flag is

Q_i(t)=0
\quad\text{if}\quad
(t-t_{\mathrm{cal},i})>\tau_{\mathrm{cal}}
\ \text{or}\
|\hat\theta_i-\theta_{\mathrm{nbr}}|
>
\kappa\sqrt{\sigma_i^2+\sigma_{\mathrm{nbr}}^2+\sigma_s^2},

where \theta_{\mathrm{nbr}} is a trust-weighted neighbour estimate and \sigma_s^2 is legitimate spatial variance. Degraded nodes are excluded from cooperation and actuation.

A dedicated model-validation sub-study is embedded in Phases 1–2: a subset of sentinel trees is instrumented with lysimeters or gravimetric ground-truth alongside the capacitance probes, and the bias and variance of the depletion model are quantified against ground truth. Without this, the CBF antecedent is untested even if the water-savings contrast succeeds.

