4. Cooperation and Fusion

4.1 Trust-weighted consensus

Let the sensor network be a time-varying directed graph \mathcal{G}(t)=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E}(t)), |\mathcal{V}|=N. Each node maintains an estimate \hat\theta_i(t). The consensus update is

\bar\theta_i(t+1)
=
\hat\theta_i(t)
+
\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_i(t)}
w_{ij}(t)\bigl(\hat\theta_j(t)-\hat\theta_i(t)\bigr),

with

w_{ij}(t)
\propto
\frac{
Q_j(t)\exp\left(-\frac{d_{ij}^2}{2\ell^2}\right)
}{
\sigma_j^2(t)
},
\qquad
\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_i(t)\cup\{i\}}w_{ij}(t)=1,
\qquad
w_{ii}(t)=1-\sum_{j\in\mathcal{N}_i(t)}w_{ij}(t).

Transmission is event-triggered:

\text{transmit at }t
\iff
|\hat\theta_i-\hat\theta_i^{\mathrm{last}}|>\Delta_\theta
\ \text{or}\
a_i(t)>a^*,

subject to

\sum_{k\in\text{hour}}\mathrm{ToA}(\mathrm{SF}_k,\mathrm{PL}_k)\leq\delta\cdot3600\ \mathrm{s}.

Theorem 1 (Consensus convergence under bounded perturbations). If \mathcal{G}(t) is jointly strongly connected over bounded intervals of length T, and Q_j=1 for at least a connected dominating subset of nodes, then the disagreement vector

\mathbf{v}(t)=\bar{\boldsymbol\theta}(t)-\theta_\infty^*\mathbf{1}

satisfies

\|\mathbf{v}(t+T)\|\leq\lambda\|\mathbf{v}(t)\|,
\qquad
\lambda=1-\eta<1,

with \eta determined by the Fiedler value of the aggregated graph over [t,t+T]. Under additive observation noise bounded by \|\mathbf{e}\|_\infty\leq\epsilon,

\limsup_{t\to\infty}\|\mathbf{v}(t)\|
\leq
\frac{C\epsilon}{1-\lambda}.

Proof sketch. Write the update as \bar{\boldsymbol\theta}(t+1)=\mathbf{W}(t)\bar{\boldsymbol\theta}(t)+\mathbf{e}(t), where \mathbf{W}(t) is row-stochastic. Joint strong connectivity over [t,t+T) makes the product

\mathbf{P}(t,t+T)=\mathbf{W}(t+T-1)\cdots\mathbf{W}(t)

primitive. The ergodicity coefficient of a primitive stochastic matrix is strictly less than one, so \mathbf{P}(t,t+T) is a contraction in \ell_\infty with factor \lambda<1. Bounded additive noise then gives input-to-state stability of the disagreement dynamics and the ultimate bound above. The trust weight Q_j removes degraded columns; if the remaining valid nodes form a connected dominating set, primitivity is preserved. \square

4.2 Centralised weighted least-squares comparator

The consensus protocol is solving a distributed spatial-fusion problem with trust weights w_{ij}. The appropriate comparator is therefore the one-step centralised version of the same fusion, not a Gaussian process with a different observation model.

Define the centralised spatial fusion at node i's location as

\hat\theta_{\mathrm{central}}(s_i)
=
\frac{
\sum_{j=1}^N Q_j \exp\left(-\frac{d_{ij}^2}{2\ell^2}\right)\sigma_j^{-2}\,\hat\theta_j
}{
\sum_{j=1}^N Q_j \exp\left(-\frac{d_{ij}^2}{2\ell^2}\right)\sigma_j^{-2}
}.

This is the closed-form weighted least-squares solution under the same kernel, trust weights, and observation variances as the consensus. The consensus iterates to a neighbour-restricted version of this fixed point. The comparison is therefore:

\mathrm{RMSE}_{\mathrm{distributed}}
\quad\text{versus}\quad
\mathrm{RMSE}_{\mathrm{central}}.

The decentralisation kill criterion is

\mathrm{RMSE}_{\mathrm{distributed}}<0.9\,\mathrm{RMSE}_{\mathrm{central}}
\quad\text{and}\quad
E_{\mathrm{distributed}}<2E_{\mathrm{central}}.

A secondary spatial upper bound is provided by a weighted Gaussian process with heteroscedastic noise,

\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathrm{diag}\left(\sigma_j^2/Q_j\right),
\qquad
\hat\theta_{\mathrm{WGP}}(s^*)=\mathbf{k}(s^*)^\top(\mathbf{K}+\mathbf{\Sigma})^{-1}\mathbf{y},

using the Matérn-5/2 covariance

k(s,s')
=
\sigma^2
\left(
1+\frac{\sqrt5 r}{\ell}+\frac{5r^2}{3\ell^2}
\right)
\exp\left(-\frac{\sqrt5 r}{\ell}\right),
\qquad
r=\|s-s'\|.

The weighted GP is reported as an upper bound on what centralised spatial information can achieve; it is not the primary comparator, because it uses a richer spatial model than the consensus and would fail the gate for the wrong reason. The centralised computation scales as \mathcal{O}(n^3), whereas decentralised consensus scales as \mathcal{O}(d\cdot n) per iteration. If the kill criterion is not satisfied, Track A reverts to gateway aggregation.

Proposal (operational). The measured disagreement norm is plotted over time against the predicted ISS bound C\epsilon/(1-\lambda), using the measured graph connectivity and the co-located-sensor noise estimate. If the measured disagreement exceeds the bound, either joint connectivity is violated (diagnosable from graph logs) or the noise model is misspecified. This turns Theorem 1 from a paper result into a field-validated diagnostic.

