5. Irrigation Dynamics and Safety Certification

5.1 Water balance and probabilistic decision

Landscape evapotranspiration and root-zone depletion follow

\mathrm{ET}_{L,i}=K_{L,i}\mathrm{ET}_0,
\qquad
K_{L,i}=k_sk_{mc}k_d,

D_i(t+1)
=
\mathrm{clip}\left[
D_i(t)-(P_{\mathrm{eff}}+I_i+\mathrm{CR}_i)
+\mathrm{ET}_{L,i}+\mathrm{DP}_i,\;
0,\;
\mathrm{TAW}_i
\right].

A scalar Kalman-like update assimilates calibrated sensor information:

D_i^+=D_i^-+K_i(D_{\theta,i}-D_i^-),
\qquad
K_i=\frac{s_{\mathrm{mod}}^2}{s_{\mathrm{mod}}^2+s_\theta^2}.

The probabilistic decision rule is

\text{irrigate iff }
\Pr\left[D_i(t)\geq \mathrm{MAD}\cdot\mathrm{TAW}_i\mid\mathrm{data}\right]\geq p^*
\ \text{and}\
V_i(t)=\text{empty},

subject to

0\leq I_i\leq I_{\max},
\qquad
t-t_{\mathrm{last}}\geq\Delta_{\min},
\qquad
\sum_{\mathrm{week}}I_i\leq W_{\max}.

The veto set is

V_i:
\quad
\text{rain forecast}\geq\rho^*,
\quad
\hat\theta_i\geq\theta_{\mathrm{ub}},
\quad
Q_i=0,
\quad
\text{human override}.

A soil-moisture decline alone is never an irrigation trigger. Phases 1–3 operate in recommendation mode only; valve actuation begins only under municipal authorisation and the governance kernel.

5.2 Control Barrier Function

Let D_i(t) be root-zone depletion and I_i(t)\in[0,I_{\max}] the irrigation input. Define the barrier

h(D_i)=(\mathrm{MAD}\cdot\mathrm{TAW}_i-D_i)D_i.

For deployment robustness, use the shifted barrier

h_\epsilon(D_i)=h(D_i)-\epsilon,
\qquad
\epsilon>0,

so that h_\epsilon(D_i)\geq0 enforces a strict interior margin. The safe set is

\mathcal{C}
=
\{D_i:\;0\leq D_i\leq \mathrm{MAD}\cdot\mathrm{TAW}_i\}.

The discrete-time CBF condition is

h_\epsilon(D_i(t+1))\geq(1-\gamma)h_\epsilon(D_i(t)),
\qquad
\gamma\in(0,1].

Substituting the water balance yields an affine inequality in I_i, enforced by the real-time quadratic program

I_i^*
=
\arg\min_{I_i}(I_i-I_{\mathrm{desired}})^2
\quad
\text{s.t.}
\quad
\text{CBF condition},
\quad
0\leq I_i\leq I_{\max}.

Theorem 2 (Forward invariance). If h_\epsilon(D_i(t))\geq0 and the CBF condition holds, then h_\epsilon(D_i(t+k))\geq0 for all k\geq0.

Proof. By induction. If h_\epsilon(D_i(t))\geq0, then

h_\epsilon(D_i(t+1))\geq(1-\gamma)h_\epsilon(D_i(t))\geq0.

Iterating gives h_\epsilon(D_i(t+k))\geq(1-\gamma)^kh_\epsilon(D_i(t))\geq0. \square

5.3 Interval-robust safety verification

The CBF is stated in terms of a point estimate D_i. In the field, D_i is inferred from \hat\theta_i with variance \sigma_{\theta,i}^2. A certificate satisfied at the estimate may be violated at the true state. Intervals are therefore propagated.

A closed interval I=[\underline{x},\overline{x}] represents bounds, with membership x\in I meaning \underline{x}\leq x\leq\overline{x}. Addition and subtraction are

[a,b]+[c,d]=[a+c,b+d],
\qquad
[a,b]-[c,d]=[a-d,b-c].

The uncertainty interval is

I(\mu,\sigma^2,\kappa)
=
\left[
\mu-\kappa\sqrt{\sigma^2},
\;
\mu+\kappa\sqrt{\sigma^2}
\right].

The interval enclosure of post-actuation depletion is

I_D(t+1)
=
\left(
I_D(t)-(I_{P_{\mathrm{eff}}}+[I_i,I_i]+I_{\mathrm{CR}})
\right)
+I_{\mathrm{ET}_L}+I_{\mathrm{DP}}.

The robust CBF condition requires

h_\epsilon(I_D(t+1).\mathrm{lo})
\geq
(1-\gamma)\min\{h_\epsilon(I_D(t).\mathrm{lo}),h_\epsilon(I_D(t).\mathrm{hi})\},

h_\epsilon(I_D(t+1).\mathrm{hi})
\geq
(1-\gamma)\min\{h_\epsilon(I_D(t).\mathrm{lo}),h_\epsilon(I_D(t).\mathrm{hi})\}.

Lemma (Concavity endpoint reduction). Because h_\epsilon(D)=(\mathrm{MAD}\cdot\mathrm{TAW}-D)D-\epsilon is strictly concave, for any closed interval [D_{\mathrm{lo}},D_{\mathrm{hi}}] and constant c,

h_\epsilon(D_{\mathrm{lo}})\geq c
\;\text{and}\;
h_\epsilon(D_{\mathrm{hi}})\geq c
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
h_\epsilon(D)\geq c
\ \forall D\in[D_{\mathrm{lo}},D_{\mathrm{hi}}].

Proof. A strictly concave function attains its minimum on a compact interval at an endpoint. Hence

h_\epsilon(D)\geq\min\{h_\epsilon(D_{\mathrm{lo}}),h_\epsilon(D_{\mathrm{hi}})\}\geq c.

\square

Theorem 3 (Robust forward safety invariance). If the current depletion interval lies inside the safe set,

h_\epsilon(I_D(t).\mathrm{lo})\geq0,
\qquad
h_\epsilon(I_D(t).\mathrm{hi})\geq0,

and the robust CBF conditions hold, then for every true physical state D_{\mathrm{true}}\in I_D(t) and every environmental flux within its interval,

h_\epsilon(D_{\mathrm{true}}(t+1))\geq0.

Proof. By interval-arithmetic soundness, D_{\mathrm{true}}(t+1)\in I_D(t+1). The robust CBF conditions imply h_\epsilon(I_D(t+1).\mathrm{lo})\geq0 and h_\epsilon(I_D(t+1).\mathrm{hi})\geq0. The concavity lemma then gives h_\epsilon(D_{\mathrm{true}}(t+1))\geq0. \square

Status. Theorem 3 is proved on paper. The interval-arithmetic module and the concavity endpoint lemma are specified in Lean 4 but are not yet fully mechanised; the remaining obligations are identified in Section 7.4. The interval construction is a control parameter, not a coverage guarantee. The choice of \kappa is pre-registered: the primary analysis uses \kappa=2.0; sensitivity is reported at \kappa\in\{1.5,2.0,2.5,3.0\}; the actuation fallback is pre-specified: if the feasible interval I_D(t+1) collapses for \kappa=2.0, the node reverts to recommendation mode for that cycle and flags a calibration event. The threshold for "collapse" is a pre-registered minimum interval width w_{\min}, fixed by power analysis on the sensor-variance decomposition, not by ARBOR data.

Two consequences follow. First, the runtime verifier evaluates h_\epsilon at two scalar endpoints rather than optimising over an uncertainty ellipsoid, so verification is O(1) per node. Second, as \sigma_{\theta,i}^2 grows, the interval widens, the feasible set of I_i shrinks, and the node degrades gracefully to human-supervised or fallback operation.

