6. Plant-Reservoir Ablation

To test whether a living tree acts as a physical reservoir computing substrate, formulate the coupled electro-hydraulic dynamics. Let

x(t)=[\Psi(t),J_s(t),V_m(t)]^\top,

where \Psi is leaf/stem water potential, J_s is sap flux density, and V_m is trans-stem bioelectric potential. The environmental input is

z(t)=[\mathrm{PAR}(t),T_{\mathrm{air}}(t),\mathrm{RH}(t),\theta(t)]^\top.

The continuous-time nonlinear state-space equations are

\frac{d\Psi}{dt}
=
-\frac{1}{C_H(\Psi)}
\left[
J_s(\Psi,\theta)-Q_{\mathrm{root}}(\theta,\Psi)
\right]
+w_1(z(t)),

\frac{dJ_s}{dt}
=
-\frac{1}{\tau_s}J_s
+\frac{k_s}{\tau_s}\Delta\Psi(z(t))
+w_2(z(t)),

\frac{dV_m}{dt}
=
-\frac{1}{R_mC_m}V_m
+I_{\mathrm{ion}}(J_s,\Psi)
+\sum_k\alpha_kz_k(t).

The reservoir state is mapped to a target y(t) via a linear readout:

\hat y(t)=\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}}\cdot\Phi(x(t)),

where \Phi is a fixed feature expansion and \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{out}} is trained by ridge regression on a training split.

The five-model hierarchy is:

Model Input Computation Purpose
M1 Environmental z Linear regression Baseline
M2 Environmental z ESN or LSTM Conventional ML
M3 z + physiological u ESN or LSTM Information gain test
M4 z Plant physical reservoir Computation test
M5 z+u Plant physical reservoir Combined test

The information claim is tested by

\Delta_{32}=1-\frac{\mathrm{RMSE}_{M3}}{\mathrm{RMSE}_{M2}},

with \Delta_{32}<0.05 implying no predictive value from physiological signals. The computation claim is tested by

\Delta_{43}=1-\frac{\mathrm{RMSE}_{M4}}{\mathrm{RMSE}_{M3}},

with \Delta_{43}<0.05 implying no computational advantage of the biological substrate over a conventional recurrent model using the same information.

Four-outcome decision map. Each combination of M3 and M4 outcomes, crossed with the dead-plant control, maps to a pre-specified architectural decision:

M3 M4 Dead-plant control Interpretation Decision
Succeeds Succeeds Fails Biological computation Integrate if gate passes
Succeeds Succeeds Succeeds Passive physical dynamics No biological claim; report as conventional ML with physiological inputs
Succeeds Fails — Information real, substrate not computational Use physiological signals in conventional model; no reservoir track
Fails Succeeds Fails Computational substrate real, information not independently useful Investigate; do not integrate
Fails Fails — No physiological value Drop Track C

The dead-plant control is decisive. It is pre-specified as a stem-cut plant with the same sensor placement, same soil, same irrigation schedule, and same electrode configuration, measured over the same duration as the live-plant treatment. Randomised physiological inputs (phase-randomised surrogates preserving autocorrelation) are a secondary control and do not substitute for the physical dead-plant test.

Sentinel sample size for H4 is increased relative to the water experiment: 8–10 sentinel trees per arm across sites, instrumented with leaf-thickness sensors at the density used in the published plant-reservoir demonstration. With fewer than 8 per arm, H4 is labelled exploratory and underpowered, and no integration decision is made on its basis.

