8. Tree-Health Non-Inferiority and Coupling Validation

8.1 Composite health index with measurement error

Water savings cannot be accepted independently of tree health. Define the composite health index

H=\sum_{i=1}^k w_ih_i,

where h_i are standardised health indicators and w_i are weights established a priori. Indicators include predawn water potential \Psi_{\mathrm{pd}}, midday stem water potential \Psi_{\mathrm{md}}, trunk diameter increment, canopy transparency, leaf area index, and mortality. Each indicator carries an instrument-specific measurement-error variance \tau_i^2, estimated from the sentinel subset where invasive ground truth is available. The composite is tested with a measurement-error-adjusted non-inferiority procedure: the observed effect is attenuated by the reliability ratio \rho_i=\sigma_{\mathrm{true},i}^2/(\sigma_{\mathrm{true},i}^2+\tau_i^2), and the non-inferiority confidence interval is constructed on the attenuated scale.

A non-inferiority margin of 0.3\ \mathrm{MPa} in \Psi_{\mathrm{pd}} and a 20\% reduction in annual trunk increment are placeholders. They are fixed by a simulation-based power analysis using variance components from the literature (or from Phase 1 pilot data): given the tree-level and zone-level intraclass correlations, the detectable margin at 80\% power and one-sided \alpha=0.025 is computed, and the margin is set to the larger of the ecological relevance threshold and the detectable margin. If the detectable margin exceeds the ecological relevance threshold, the design is enlarged or H3 is reframed as a superiority test on water use with a bounded health-monitoring rule, not a formal non-inferiority claim.

Non-inferiority is demonstrated only if, for all indicators,

\text{lower bound of }95\%\ \mathrm{CI}\text{ for }(H_{\mathrm{ARBOR}}-H_{\mathrm{control}})>-\delta_{H,i}.

Any statistically significant increase in mortality triggers stopping rules regardless of \delta_H.

8.2 Coupling validation

The governance kernel proves that if the water-balance model and sensor intervals are correct, actuation preserves the hydrological safe set. The tree-health non-inferiority test is a statistical procedure on ecological indicators. These two safety cases operate on different objects—depletion D versus predawn water potential \Psi_{\mathrm{pd}}—and the formal model does not contain a map from one to the other.

Hypothesis H7 (Coupling). The CBF safe set for depletion is sufficient for ecological safety: there exists a function g and a noise term \eta with \mathbb{E}[\eta]=0 such that

\Psi_{\mathrm{pd}} = g(D,\mathbf{z})+\eta,

and h_\epsilon(D)\geq0 implies

\Pr\left[\Psi_{\mathrm{pd}}>-2.5\ \mathrm{MPa}\mid D,\mathbf{z}\right]\geq 1-\alpha_{\mathrm{coup}},

for a pre-registered \alpha_{\mathrm{coup}}=0.05.

Test. On sentinel trees, fit the mixed-effects model

\Psi_{\mathrm{pd},ij}=g(D_{ij},\mathbf{z}_{ij})+u_j+\eta_{ij},
\qquad
u_j\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_u^2),
\qquad
\eta_{ij}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_\eta^2),

and evaluate the lower 5\% quantile of the predicted \Psi_{\mathrm{pd}} at the CBF boundary h_\epsilon(D)=0. If the lower quantile is below -2.5\ \mathrm{MPa}, H7 fails and the governance kernel's ecological claim is not supported, regardless of the formal proof. This is the single most important empirical test in the programme, because it is the only place where the formal hydrological safety case and the ecological non-inferiority case are explicitly coupled.

