10. Experimental Programme

10.1 Phase 0: Prior art, energy audit, and kernel verification

Systematic literature review across urban tree water management, distributed environmental sensing, plant electrophysiology, plant microbial fuel cells, and reservoir computing. Bench measurement of E_{\mathrm{cyc}} components and sleep current. Written power budget replacing illustrative values. Formal verification of the point-valued governance kernel; interval-robust module proof obligations closed or explicitly documented.

10.2 Phase 1: Three-node laboratory prototype and model-validation sub-study

Three independent nodes with a plant, environmental sensors, calibrated soil-moisture and temperature sensing, local storage, LoRa, and an energy-monitoring circuit. A subset of plants is instrumented with lysimeters or gravimetric ground-truth alongside the capacitance probes, to quantify the bias and variance of the depletion model against ground truth. Success criteria: continuous operation for \geq3 months without battery depletion; packet delivery ratio >90\%; soil-moisture correlation with gravimetric samples r>0.8; depletion-model RMSE against lysimeter ground truth below a pre-registered threshold; gateway verifier rejects all actuation proposals during recommendation mode.

10.3 Phase 2: Energy characterisation

Electrode configuration: carbon felt anode and cathode, 100\ \mathrm{cm^2} each, anode 20\ \mathrm{cm} deep, cathode 5\ \mathrm{cm} deep, external resistance 1000\ \Omega adjustable. Replication: 15 trees with PMFC per site, 15 with soil-MFC per site, 15 with PV per site, 15 with blank electrodes per site; 60 trees per site, 180 trees across 3 sites. A primary-battery comparator arm is added: 15 nodes per site with a lithium-thionyl-chloride or LiFePO_4 primary cell sized for a 5-year replacement interval, to test whether the simplest energy source dominates on lifecycle cost and maintenance. PV configuration: 10\ \mathrm{cm}\times10\ \mathrm{cm} panel, 100\ \mathrm{mW} rated, 1000\ \mathrm{mAh} LiFePO_4 battery, MPPT controller. Measurements every 15 min for \geq90 days outdoors. Evaluates H1 and the relative and absolute energy gates.

10.4 Phase 3: Multi-agent experiments

Isolated, cooperative, and centralised operation; communication interruption; node failure; injected sensor drift. The centralised comparator is the weighted least-squares fusion defined in Section 4.2, with the weighted GP reported as a secondary upper bound. Evaluates H2 and H5. Theorem 1's ISS bound is tested against measured disagreement under dropout, and the measured disagreement is plotted against the predicted bound using measured graph connectivity and co-located-sensor noise estimates.

10.5 Phase 4: Controlled water pilot

The design is a three-site, three-DBH-class, four-arm randomised block design. Very large trees (>50\ \mathrm{cm}) are excluded. The canonical design is:

Site DBH class Arms Trees per cell Trees per site
1,2,3 Small 5\text{--}15\ \mathrm{cm} 4 4 48
1,2,3 Medium 15\text{--}30\ \mathrm{cm} 4 4 48
1,2,3 Large 30\text{--}50\ \mathrm{cm} 4 4 48

Thus each site has 3\times4\times4=48 trees; the total is 144 trees; each arm has 36 trees across sites (12 per site). Sentinel instrumentation is one tree per DBH class per arm per site for the water and health endpoints, plus an additional 8–10 leaf-thickness-instrumented sentinel trees per arm across sites for the reservoir sub-study (H4).

The four arms are: (1) municipal timer as operated; (2) sensor-free weather and soil-water-balance model; (3) calibrated soil-moisture threshold with rain skip; (4) ARBOR cooperative recommendation. The strongest baseline is pre-registered as Arm 3, the conservative sensor-based arm, before Season 1 data are analysed. This eliminates the selection event that would otherwise inflate the Type I error of the Season 2 contrast. Secondary contrasts (Arm 4 versus Arms 1 and 2) are tested using a Holm–Bonferroni procedure within the family of three pairwise comparisons, preserving familywise error at one-sided \alpha=0.025.

The primary analysis model is

W_{ijk}
=
\mu+\tau_i+Z_j+T_{k(j)}+\beta^\top\mathbf{x}_{ijk}+\varepsilon_{ijk},

where \tau_i is the treatment effect, Z_j\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_Z^2) is a zone random effect, T_{k(j)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_T^2) is a tree random effect nested in zone, \mathbf{x}_{ijk} are covariates, and \varepsilon_{ijk}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_\epsilon^2). The primary contrast is Arm 4 versus Arm 3, estimated by REML with Kenward–Roger or Satterthwaite denominator degrees of freedom. Non-inferiority is tested one-sided at \alpha=0.025 with Holm–Bonferroni within the indicator family, using the measurement-error-adjusted composite of Section 8.1. The coupling validation of Section 8.2 is fitted on the sentinel subset and reported as a pre-registered secondary analysis.

10.6 Phase 5: Urban pilot

Conducted only after Phases 1–4, in a green space, integrated with a municipal interface. Evaluates H6 if the integration gate is passed.

10.7 Stopping rules

Any tree death attributable to treatment: pause experiment and review protocol. \Psi_{\mathrm{pd}}<-2.5\ \mathrm{MPa}: emergency irrigation and flag tree. >20\% mortality in any treatment: stop experiment and analyse failure. Sensor failure rate >20\%: pause data collection and repair sensors. Governance-kernel proof failure on a deployed build: halt actuation and revert to recommendation mode. H7 failure: revert to recommendation mode pending model revision.

