Appendix A — Corrections and Normative Clarifications

A.1 LoRa time-on-air: the dominant energy term is spreading factor, not transmit duration

The illustrative budgets in early drafts assumed a fixed 2 s transmit duration. That is incorrect and materially changes the energy conclusion for short uplinks. For a 20-byte application payload at 125 kHz bandwidth, coding rate 4/5, and 8-symbol preamble, the measured time-on-air is approximately 56.6 ms at SF7 and 1,318.9 ms at SF12. Each step up in spreading factor roughly doubles airtime. At SF12, a 20-byte packet occupies the channel for more than one second, which has direct duty-cycle and energy consequences.

The corrected consequence is that the node energy budget must be stated per operating profile, not as a single number. Appendix D provides these profiles. Any claim of the form “bioenergy can power the node” must specify spreading factor, payload size, uplink interval, storage efficiency, and the 30-day minimum observed output—not the mean alone.

A.2 PMFC power density is an operating-envelope quantity

Reported plant microbial fuel cell power densities span a wide range because they are measured under different conditions. A green-infrastructure study reported peak power densities of 11 mW/m² in summer and 23 mW/m² in winter for PMFCs integrated into recycled-material green infrastructure. Laboratory studies with optimised anodes report higher values—260 mW/m² over 35 days for an Aloe vera PMFC with a carbonised Ipomoea carnea anode, and 127 mW/m² for a Cymbopogon citratus configuration with a 7.5 cm inter-electrode distance, with a reported minimum of 16.90 mW/m² even in the absence of sunlight. Dracaena-based PMFCs with municipal solid waste compost reached 204 mW/m².

These values are not interchangeable. A polarisation-curve maximum measured over minutes is not a sustained power supply. Appendix D therefore requires sustained-load reporting as the primary metric, with the 10th percentile and minimum reported alongside the mean.

A.3 Solar is the default because of worst-week behaviour, not mean-day superiority

Solar panels supply far more mean energy than an ultra-low-power node requires. The correct design statement is not that solar is “free” or “95× oversized,” but that solar’s worst-week output still exceeds the node’s worst-week demand by a comfortable margin, whereas bioenergy’s worst-week output can fall to near zero. Appendix D.5 specifies the worst-week sizing rule.

A.4 Receive windows, retries, and joins must be in the energy budget

LoRaWAN Class A nodes open receive windows after uplink. Retries, acknowledgements, ADR changes, joins and rejoins, and firmware updates add airtime and energy that are absent from datasheet-only budgets. Recent work on LoRaWAN energy consumption under duty-cycle limitation explicitly models retries and acknowledgements as first-order energy terms. Appendix F therefore imposes a radio overhead budget and a hard rule: no protocol may assume more than one uplink per 15 minutes per node at SF12, or more than one uplink per 5 minutes at SF7, without a site-specific duty-cycle and collision analysis.

A.5 “Biohybrid sensing” is the defensible term

Using a biological system as a sensor, signal source, or auxiliary power source is defensible. Claiming that the biological system performs the computation that should be attributed to the electronic readout, model, or control policy is not defensible without the ablation tests in Appendix H. The term biohybrid sensing is used throughout these appendices unless H4 has passed its pre-registered gate.

A.6 Urban-tree water figures are not directly comparable across sources

Maintenance recommendations, rule-of-thumb estimates, modelled savings, and pilot baselines are different quantities. They must not be averaged. The correct conversion is the water-balance method of Appendix G: litres per tree per day = ET_c (mm/day) × effective wetted or canopy area (m²), with soil-water storage as the buffer.

