Appendix B — Definitions and Measurement Hierarchy

B.1 Measurement classes (normative)

Class Definition Examples Permitted use in irrigation decisions
Direct Quantity measured by the instrument’s primary physical principle, with traceable calibration Gravimetric soil water content; lysimeter mass change; metered irrigation volume; rain-gauge depth May trigger irrigation directly, subject to the safety envelope
Calibrated proxy Instrument responds to a related physical property; converted via a soil- or species-specific calibration with stated uncertainty Capacitive soil moisture → volumetric water content; thermal dissipation → sap flux density; dendrometer → stem radius change May trigger irrigation only if calibration age, temperature compensation, and uncertainty are within pre-registered bounds
Model-derived Quantity computed from measurements plus a model whose parameters may not transfer across sites FAO-56 ET_c; reservoir-computed transpiration; ML stress score May recommend irrigation; may not trigger irrigation autonomously unless validated against a direct or calibrated-proxy reference on held-out data
Biological signal Voltage, impedance, or other electrical signal whose mapping to a physiological state is not uniquely identified Plant electrome features; raw PMFC voltage May trigger a human-review alert or a data-quality check; may not trigger irrigation autonomously

Rule. A model-derived estimate or biological signal may never silently promote itself to a direct measurement in logs, dashboards, or control decisions. The class must be stored with every value.

B.2 Required metadata for every reading

Each stored reading carries: node ID, sensor ID, measurement class, timestamp (UTC and local), raw value, calibrated value, calibration ID and calibration date, temperature at the sensor, battery or supercapacitor voltage, firmware version, and a data-quality flag. A reading without calibration metadata is not a measurement; it is an uncalibrated signal and must be labelled as such.

B.3 Uncertainty language (normative)

Accuracy is closeness to a reference (gravimetric water content, lysimeter mass, metered volume). Precision is repeatability under identical conditions. Drift is systematic change in the raw-to-calibrated mapping over time. Bias is mean signed error against the reference. RMSE is root-mean-square error against the reference on held-out data. Accuracy must not be reported from training data; R² must not be reported without RMSE and bias; and a sensor must not be described as “±2%” without stating whether the figure refers to volumetric water content, relative error, or raw ADC counts.

