D.4 PMFC and BPV characterisation protocol (Track B)

Duration. Minimum 90 days outdoors, covering at least one dry spell and one wet spell. Report weekly.

Measurements. Minimum 15-minute interval (hourly acceptable for slow cells): cell voltage, current through a fixed load, power, anode and cathode temperature, soil or substrate moisture, air temperature, light (PAR or lux), and rainfall. Log raw voltage and current, not power alone.

Tests.

1. Open-circuit voltage. Daily, 1 minute, no load.
2. Sustained-load power. At the maximum-power-point load, continuously for 7 days, reported as mean, 10th percentile, and minimum.
3. Polarisation curve. Weekly, clearly labelled as short-duration, not sustained power.
4. Storage test. Charge a supercapacitor or battery from the cell alone; report time to charge from 20% to 80% and usable energy delivered.
5. Disturbance test. Log irrigation, fertilisation, and soil disturbance events; report recovery time to 90% of pre-event power.

Gate for promotion (pre-register one).

· Auxiliary or trickle gate: bioenergy supplies at least 20% of measured node demand, averaged over 30 consecutive days, with 10th-percentile daily supply at least 10% of demand.
· Primary gate: bioenergy supplies at least 80% of measured node demand over 30 consecutive days, with 10th-percentile daily supply at least 60% of demand, and no maintenance visit required during the period.

If neither gate passes, bioenergy remains a sidecar experiment. A failed gate is a result, not a reason to change the gate after seeing the data.

D.5 Solar sizing rule (Track A)

Size the panel and storage for the worst week, not the mean day. Estimate worst-week solar energy from local historical data for the deployment month, including cloud cover, shade, orientation, and dust. Require:

E_{\mathrm{solar,worst\ week}} \geq 2\,E_{\mathrm{node,worst\ week}},

including 50% overhead, and

\mathrm{storage\ capacity} \geq 7\ \mathrm{days\ of\ node\ energy}

at the deployment profile, with depth-of-discharge and temperature derating applied. Log panel voltage, charge current, and battery voltage daily. A node that enters low-power mode more than twice per month is undersized or shaded and must be flagged.

D.6 Lifecycle and maintenance ledger (per node, per year)

Report bill of materials; installation time; arborist time (if any); site visits and reason; electrode or substrate replacement; sensor recalibration events; battery or supercapacitor replacement; data yield (received ÷ expected readings); cost per tree per year; and cost per litre saved with the baseline stated. A system without this ledger is not deployable, however good its laboratory RMSE.

