Appendix E — Sensing: Calibration, Uncertainty, and Reference Instruments

E.1 Soil-moisture calibration protocol (normative)

Laboratory calibration. For each soil texture class in the deployment, build a calibration curve from at least 5 known volumetric water contents (oven-dry to near-saturation), with 3 replicates each. Fit a monotonic curve (linear or quadratic; report R², RMSE, and bias). Store the curve ID with the node.

Field check. At installation, take a gravimetric sample adjacent to the sensor (within 10 cm, same depth, same hour). If the field check disagrees with the sensor by more than the pre-registered tolerance (recommended: 0.03 m³/m³), recalibrate or flag the node.

Temperature compensation. Log soil temperature with every moisture reading. Apply the temperature correction from the calibration. If the correction exceeds 0.02 m³/m³, flag the reading as temperature-sensitive.

Salinity check. In fertilised or saline soils, measure electrical conductivity at installation and monthly. If EC exceeds the calibration range, the moisture reading is not valid for irrigation decisions until recalibrated.

Recalibration interval. Pre-register (recommended: 90 days, or 30 days in saline or fertilised soils). A node past its interval is degraded by definition, not by exception.

Drift detection. Compare each node’s moisture trajectory against at least two neighbours in similar soil. A persistent offset greater than 0.04 m³/m³ for more than 7 days triggers a field check.

E.2 Soil-water accounting (normative)

Field capacity is conventionally the water content at −33 kPa (−0.33 bar, pF 2.5), after rapid drainage has finished. Permanent wilting point is −1500 kPa (−15 bar, pF 4.2). Plant-available water is the difference between field capacity and wilting point.

Texture (illustrative) Field capacity (vol.) Wilting point (vol.) Available water, 30 cm root zone Readily available at 50% depletion
Sand 0.07 0.03 12 mm 6 mm
Sandy loam 0.16 0.07 27 mm 14 mm
Loam 0.27 0.13 42 mm 21 mm
Clay loam 0.33 0.18 45 mm 23 mm

Rule. Use site-measured field capacity and wilting point where available. The table above is for planning only. Irrigation thresholds must be expressed as depletion fraction (for example, irrigate at 50% depletion of available water) plus a soil-moisture upper bound (for example, never irrigate above 90% of field capacity), not as a raw sensor voltage.

E.3 Sap-flow protocol (sentinel trees only)

Maximum density. Pre-register: at most two probe pairs per tree, at most one tree in five instrumented as a sentinel, and no probes on trees with trunk diameter below 10 cm without arborist approval.

Calibration. Species-specific calibration against a gravimetric or lysimeter reference on cut stems or potted trees before field deployment. Report the calibration equation, RMSE, and bias.

Wound correction. The formation of wounds around thermal-dissipation probes leads to underestimation of sap flux density by 21.4 \pm 3\% in beech and 47.5 \pm 3.8\% in oak. An uncorrected sap-flow value is not a measurement of transpiration. Report the wound-effect correction explicitly, together with its uncertainty.

Reporting. Report sap flux density, estimated sap flow (L/day), and the uncertainty interval. Sap flow must never be reported as a single point estimate without uncertainty.

Removal. Pre-register a removal and replacement interval and a wound-assessment protocol. A sentinel tree showing decline attributable to probing must be removed from the study and reported as an adverse event.

E.4 Plant electrophysiology protocol (Track C only)

Electrodes. Pre-register electrode type, placement, contact medium, and impedance check. Report contact impedance at installation and weekly.

Controls. Every electrophysiology experiment must include a simultaneous environmental control (soil moisture, air temperature, humidity, light) and a mechanical-disturbance control (a sham handling event). A signal that cannot be distinguished from handling, temperature, or light change is not a stress signal.

Analysis. Pre-register features (amplitude, duration, frequency-band power) and the classifier. Features must not be selected on the test set.

Decision use. Electrophysiology may trigger a human-review alert or a data-quality check. It may not trigger irrigation autonomously until it passes the ablation test in Appendix H and the water gate in Appendix I.

E.5 Reference instruments (what counts as ground truth)

Quantity Reference instrument Frequency
Soil water content Gravimetric sampling (oven-dry) Installation, monthly spot checks, recalibration
Irrigation volume Inline flow meter (metered, not timed) Every irrigation event
Rainfall Rain gauge at site (not a distant station) Daily
Tree water status Stem water potential or pressure chamber where feasible; dendrometer plus canopy score otherwise Weekly in pilot; monthly in deployment
Transpiration (sentinel) Lysimeter or gravimetric on potted trees; sap flow with species calibration in field Continuous (sentinel only)

