F.3 Multi-agent protocols (one concrete protocol per experiment)

Protocol 1 — Gossip-averaged soil-moisture estimation. Each node broadcasts its calibrated moisture, uncertainty, and calibration age at most once per hour. Neighbours within a pre-registered radius (for example, 50 m) and the same soil class weight the estimate by inverse uncertainty and inverse distance. No neighbour may contribute more than 50% of the estimate. If fewer than two neighbours are available, the node falls back to its own reading and flags “no gossip.”

Protocol 2 — Event-triggered sharing. A node transmits immediately (subject to duty cycle) only when moisture crosses a threshold, when change exceeds a pre-registered delta in one hour, when an anomaly score exceeds a threshold, or when an irrigation event occurs. All other transmissions are batched hourly. The experiment reports messages saved versus a periodic baseline, detection delay, and missed-event rate.

Protocol 3 — Neighbour-voted anomaly detection. Each node computes a local anomaly score (for example, a z-score against its own 7-day history). If the score exceeds a threshold, it queries neighbours or the gateway for their scores. A local-only anomaly is classified as likely sensor failure; a spatially coherent anomaly as likely environmental event; no neighbour agreement as uncertain and flagged for human review. The experiment reports false-alarm rate, missed-event rate, and time-to-detection, not accuracy alone.

Protocol 4 — Gateway-outage autonomy. Nodes store 72 hours of readings and 7 days of threshold rules locally. During an outage, nodes continue threshold irrigation from local rules. On reconnection, nodes backfill in chronological order with duplicate suppression. The experiment reports data loss, irrigation events during the outage, and whether any safety-envelope violation occurred.

F.4 Cooperation-gain metrics (normative)

Report all of: held-out RMSE (moisture, transpiration, or water use, as applicable), bias, false-alarm rate, missed-event rate, time-to-detection, airtime per node per day, and energy per node per day. A cooperation gain that requires more than twice the airtime or twice the energy for less than 10% RMSE improvement is not a deployable gain. The trade-off must be stated explicitly.

