Appendix G — Irrigation Decision Logic, Safety Envelope, and the Concavity Proof

G.1 FAO-56 reference evapotranspiration (normative baseline)

The FAO-56 Penman–Monteith equation is the standard for reference evapotranspiration ET_0:

\mathrm{ET}_0=
\frac{
0.408\Delta(R_n-G)+\gamma\frac{900}{T+273}u_2(e_s-e_a)
}{
\Delta+\gamma(1+0.34u_2)
},

where ET_0 is in mm/day, R_n is net radiation, G is soil heat flux, T is mean air temperature at 2 m, u_2 is wind speed at 2 m, e_s-e_a is the saturation vapour-pressure deficit, \Delta is the slope of the vapour-pressure curve, and \gamma is the psychrometric constant. Crop evapotranspiration is

\mathrm{ET}_c=K_c\,\mathrm{ET}_0.

A worked planning example: ET_0=5 mm/day and K_c=0.7 give ET_c=3.5 mm/day. For a 1 m² wetted pit, that is 3.5 L/day; for a 4 m² canopy or pit area, 14 L/day. Actual irrigation is ET_c minus effective rainfall minus soil-water depletion, capped by the safety envelope below. The example is for planning only; site-measured K_c or a pre-registered tree coefficient with uncertainty must replace it.

G.2 Water-balance equation (normative)

\text{Irrigation need}
=
\mathrm{ET}_c
-
\text{effective rainfall}
-
\Delta(\text{soil water storage}),

where \Delta(\text{soil water storage}) is the change in plant-available water in the root zone, estimated from calibrated soil-moisture sensors. All three terms must be reported separately. A system that reports only “water saved” without rainfall and soil-water terms is not reporting a water balance.

G.3 Safety envelope (normative, enforced locally on the valve controller)

Constraint Recommended default Rationale
Maximum single-event volume Pre-register per tree size (e.g. ≤20 L for a young street tree) Prevents waterlogging and run-off
Maximum weekly volume Pre-register (e.g. ≤40 L/week for a young tree, site-adjusted) Prevents chronic over-irrigation
Minimum interval between events ≥24 h, unless soil moisture is below the wilting-point threshold and an arborist approves Prevents rapid cycling
Soil-moisture upper bound Never irrigate if moisture exceeds 90% of field capacity Prevents waterlogging
Rain skip Skip if ≥5 mm rain in the past 24 h or ≥10 mm forecast in the next 24 h (site-tuned) Avoids irrigating into rain
Night and wind rule Prefer early morning; avoid high-wind periods if run-off risk is high Reduces evaporation and drift
Human override Any authorised human may pause irrigation for a node, cluster, or site Safety and municipal control
Fail-safe On loss of communication for more than 72 h, fall back to the local threshold rule; on sensor failure, fall back to FAO-56 timer with safety caps Prevents autonomous error accumulation

Rule. The valve controller must refuse any command violating the envelope, log the refusal, and alert. A refused command is a safety success, not a system failure.

G.4 Baselines (normative, three required)

1. Timer baseline. The municipality’s current schedule as actually operated (metered volume, not scheduled volume).
2. FAO-56 plus threshold baseline. FAO-56 ET_c plus a calibrated soil-moisture threshold, rain skip, and the safety envelope, implemented on the same hardware as Track A but without gossip, reservoir, or bioenergy.
3. ARBOR (Track A or integrated). Distributed, physiologically informed recommendation plus the safety envelope.

Report litres per tree, soil-moisture trajectory, tree-health indicators, and irrigation events separately for each baseline. Report water saved as (\text{baseline litres} - \text{ARBOR litres}) / \text{baseline litres}, with the baseline named, the period stated, rainfall stated, and the health outcome stated. A percentage without those four qualifiers is not a result.

G.5 Concavity lemma: endpoint reduction for the robust CBF (with full proof)

Lemma. Let

h_\epsilon(D)=(\mathrm{MAD}\cdot\mathrm{TAW}-D)D-\epsilon,

with \mathrm{MAD}\cdot\mathrm{TAW}>0 and \epsilon>0. Then h_\epsilon is strictly concave on \mathbb{R}. Consequently, for any closed interval [D_{\mathrm{lo}},D_{\mathrm{hi}}] and any constant c,

h_\epsilon(D_{\mathrm{lo}})\geq c
\quad\text{and}\quad
h_\epsilon(D_{\mathrm{hi}})\geq c
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
h_\epsilon(D)\geq c
\quad\forall D\in[D_{\mathrm{lo}},D_{\mathrm{hi}}].

Proof. Write M=\mathrm{MAD}\cdot\mathrm{TAW}. Then

h_\epsilon(D)=MD-D^2-\epsilon,
\qquad
h_\epsilon''(D)=-2<0,

so h_\epsilon is strictly concave. Let D\in[D_{\mathrm{lo}},D_{\mathrm{hi}}]. There exists \lambda\in[0,1] such that

D=\lambda D_{\mathrm{lo}}+(1-\lambda)D_{\mathrm{hi}}.

A direct algebraic expansion gives the identity

h_\epsilon(D)
=
\lambda h_\epsilon(D_{\mathrm{lo}})
+(1-\lambda)h_\epsilon(D_{\mathrm{hi}})
+\lambda(1-\lambda)(D_{\mathrm{lo}}-D_{\mathrm{hi}})^2.

Indeed, the squared cross-term arises from

-\left(\lambda D_{\mathrm{lo}}+(1-\lambda)D_{\mathrm{hi}}\right)^2
=
-\lambda D_{\mathrm{lo}}^2-(1-\lambda)D_{\mathrm{hi}}^2
+\lambda(1-\lambda)(D_{\mathrm{lo}}-D_{\mathrm{hi}})^2,

and the linear term M(\lambda D_{\mathrm{lo}}+(1-\lambda)D_{\mathrm{hi}}) distributes without a cross-term; the constant -\epsilon contributes -\lambda\epsilon-(1-\lambda)\epsilon=-\epsilon. Since \lambda(1-\lambda)\geq0 and (D_{\mathrm{lo}}-D_{\mathrm{hi}})^2\geq0, the last term is non-negative. Therefore

h_\epsilon(D)
\geq
\lambda h_\epsilon(D_{\mathrm{lo}})
+(1-\lambda)h_\epsilon(D_{\mathrm{hi}})
\geq
\lambda c+(1-\lambda)c
=
c.

This holds for every D\in[D_{\mathrm{lo}},D_{\mathrm{hi}}]. \square

Corollary. In the robust CBF condition, it suffices to evaluate h_\epsilon at the two endpoints I_D(t+1).\mathrm{lo} and I_D(t+1).\mathrm{hi}. If both satisfy the required lower bound, the concavity lemma guarantees that every D in the interval also satisfies it. This is the justification for the O(1) runtime verifier: two scalar evaluations replace optimisation over the uncertainty interval.

Mechanisation note. The identity above is the proof obligation barrier_concave_min_at_endpoints in the Lean development. A bare nlinarith call is not a sufficient proof description; the explicit convex-combination decomposition and the sq_nonneg hint are required. Verified interval-arithmetic libraries for Lean 4 are now available and should be reused rather than re-proving subtraction validity from scratch. LeanCert provides checked enclosures for interval arithmetic and a tactic for proving bounds on real-valued expressions. The Interval.sub validity proof follows from A.\mathrm{lo}\leq A.\mathrm{hi} and B.\mathrm{lo}\leq B.\mathrm{hi} by linarith after the observation A.\mathrm{lo}+B.\mathrm{lo}\leq A.\mathrm{hi}+B.\mathrm{hi}. The control-barrier-function interval-analysis literature confirms that interval and zonotope representations are the standard computationally efficient choice for robust CBF verification under measurement and actuation uncertainty.

