Appendix H — Reservoir Computing: Full Ablation Protocol

H.1 Claim to be tested (normative)

H4 (operational). Reservoir features derived from plant physiological dynamics improve held-out-plant prediction of transpiration or photosynthetic rate by at least 10% RMSE versus environmental sensors alone, and versus a parameter-matched conventional recurrent model, at equal or lower electronic energy.

H.2 Models (all three required)

Model Inputs Trainable parameters Notes
A — Environmental only Soil moisture, soil temperature, air temperature, humidity, light, rain Pre-register count Baseline
B — Environmental plus reservoir A inputs plus leaf-thickness or electrophysiology reservoir features Same readout size as C Test model
C — Environmental plus conventional recurrent A inputs plus conventional recurrent features (for example, echo-state or GRU) Matched to B Controls for “any memory helps”

Rule. B and C must have the same number of trainable readout parameters. If B wins only because it has more parameters, it has not shown that plant dynamics help; it has shown that more parameters help.

H.3 Evaluation (normative)

Held-out plants. Train on N-k plants, test on k held-out plants. Repeat across folds. Report mean and standard deviation across folds.

Held-out days. Within each plant, hold out entire days, not random time slices, to avoid leakage from autocorrelation.

Held-out stress events. Hold out at least one drought or heat event per fold. A model that works only on well-watered days is not a stress detector.

Metrics. RMSE, bias, and R² on held-out plants; energy per inference; latency; sensor cost per plant.

Ablation. Report B versus A, B versus C, and B versus A with shuffled reservoir features as a negative control. If shuffled features perform as well as real features, the reservoir is not providing information.

H.4 Sensor-cost accounting (normative)

Report the number of leaf-thickness sensors per plant, unit cost, installation time, failure rate, calibration interval, and maintenance visits per plant per season. The published plant-reservoir demonstration used eight leaf-thickness sensors on strawberry plants to solve environmental and eco-physiological tasks. If the reservoir requires eight sensors per plant at high cost, its RMSE gain must be weighed against the cost of simply deploying more soil-moisture sensors. A reservoir that is more expensive than the problem it solves is not a deployment result.

H.5 Decision rule (pre-register)

Pass: B beats A by at least 10% RMSE on held-out plants, and B beats C by at least 5% RMSE or matches C with at least 30% lower inference energy.

Partial: B beats A but not C. Plant dynamics help, but a conventional model captures the same information more cheaply. Report as “reservoir not needed.”

Fail: B does not beat A by at least 10%. Reservoir features do not add deployable predictive value in this configuration.

H.6 Four-outcome decision map

M3 (information) M4 (computation) Dead-plant control Interpretation Decision
Succeeds Succeeds Fails Biological computation Integrate if the integration gate passes
Succeeds Succeeds Succeeds Passive physical dynamics No biological claim; report as conventional ML with physiological inputs
Succeeds Fails — Information real, substrate not computational Use physiological signals in a conventional model; no reservoir track
Fails Succeeds Fails Computational substrate real, information not independently useful Investigate; do not integrate
Fails Fails — No physiological value Drop Track C

The dead-plant control is decisive. It is pre-specified as a stem-cut plant with the same sensor placement, same soil, same irrigation schedule, and same electrode configuration, measured over the same duration as the live-plant treatment. Randomised physiological inputs (phase-randomised surrogates preserving autocorrelation) are a secondary control and do not substitute for the physical dead-plant test.

