Appendix I — Water Pilot: Full Protocol

I.1 Design (normative)

Design. Randomised complete block design, with blocks defined by soil texture, species, and sun exposure. Minimum three replicates per treatment per block; pre-register the power analysis.

Treatments. Timer (as operated), FAO-56 plus threshold, ARBOR (Track A). Bioenergy and reservoir are not treatments in the water pilot unless they have already passed their gates.

Duration. One full growing season minimum; two seasons preferred. Report results per season, not pooled across seasons without a season effect.

Randomisation. Randomise treatment within each block. Do not assign the “best” trees to ARBOR.

I.2 Outcomes (pre-register primary versus secondary)

Outcome Type Measurement Frequency
Litres per tree Primary Metered flow Every event
Soil-moisture trajectory Primary Calibrated volumetric water content Hourly
Tree-health score Primary Pre-registered composite (canopy condition plus stem water potential or dendrometer plus growth increment) Weekly in pilot
Irrigation events Secondary Count and volume per event Every event
Data yield Secondary Received ÷ expected readings Daily
Maintenance visits Secondary Count and reason Every visit
Cost per tree Secondary Bill of materials plus labour plus maintenance End of season

Rule. The primary outcome is joint: water saved and health maintained. A treatment that saves water but degrades health is not a success. Pre-register the health non-inferiority margin (for example, health score within 10% of the FAO-56 baseline).

I.3 Analysis (normative)

Analyse by block and treatment, with the tree as the experimental unit, not the sensor reading. Report effect sizes with confidence intervals, not p-values alone. Report rainfall, ET_0, and soil-water storage as covariates. Report missing data and data-quality exclusions explicitly. A pilot that excludes 30% of nodes without explanation is not a pilot; it is a selection bias. Pre-register the analysis code or analysis plan before data collection. Label all post-hoc analyses as exploratory.

I.4 Adverse events (normative)

Report any tree decline, waterlogging, run-off complaint, valve failure, sensor-induced wound infection, or municipal complaint. Adverse events are results, not embarrassments. A pilot with zero adverse events and 100% data yield should be treated with scepticism, not celebrated.

